Do not let the panic header's assertion eat the panic.

A kernel that panics early now says only this, four times, and restarts:

    pexpert/arm/pe_init.c:557 Assertion failed: location > (char *) panic_info

That is PE_get_offset_into_panic_region() being handed debug_buf_base, from
PE_init_panicheader(), on the panic path. On embedded debug_buf_base starts
as NULL and is pointed into the panic region by debug_log_init(), which runs
from machine_init() -- kernel_bootstrap() line 367, where oslog_init() is at
line 304. Anything that panics in the sixty lines between the two arrives
with the pointer still NULL, the assertion is true, and it fires.

What it costs is the panic that was being reported. The assertion panics in
its turn, nests, and the original message never reaches the console; the
screen fills with four copies of a complaint about an uninitialised pointer
and nothing about what went wrong. The last useful line before it was
"oslog_init completed, 16 chunks, 8 io pages", and the next step was never
named, which is all anyone could tell.

So PE_init_panicheader() now returns when debug_buf_base is not yet inside
the panic region. There is no header to write before the log has somewhere
to live. The console path is separate and still prints, so the panic is
reported as it would be at any other time; only the copy in VRAM is lost,
and nothing on this handset was ever going to collect that.
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -567,6 +567,33 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up: and not before debug_log_init() has placed the log.
+	 *
+	 * debug_buf_base starts life NULL on embedded and is pointed into the
+	 * panic region by debug_log_init(), which runs from machine_init() --
+	 * line 367 of kernel_bootstrap(), while oslog_init() is at line 304.
+	 * Anything that panics in the sixty lines between the two arrives here
+	 * with debug_buf_base still NULL, and the assertion inside
+	 * PE_get_offset_into_panic_region() fires on it:
+	 *
+	 *     pe_init.c:557 Assertion failed: location > (char *) panic_info
+	 *
+	 * That is a true statement about an uninitialised pointer and a useless
+	 * thing to say, because it replaces the panic that was being reported
+	 * with one about the reporting, and then nests. The real message never
+	 * reaches the console.
+	 *
+	 * There is no header to write before the log has somewhere to live, so
+	 * there is nothing to do here but leave. The console path is separate
+	 * and still prints; only the copy in VRAM is lost, and iBoot is not
+	 * going to collect it from this handset anyway.
+	 */
+	if (debug_buf_base == NULL ||
+	    debug_buf_base <= (char *)panic_info) {
+		return;
+	}
+
 	bzero(panic_info, sizeof(struct embedded_panic_header));
 
 	/*
